WEBVTT

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:08.340
The sun is our nearest star. It provides us with warmth and light.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:15.060
We all know that the sun is important to life on Earth, but few of us have been given a good description of the sun and its composition.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:21.180
Our sun is an average star, similar to millions of others in the universe, but it's a big energy machine.

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:29.960
If you could capture the energy the sun produces in one second, that would supply the United States with enough energy for the next 13 billion years.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.670
Where does the sun's power come from?

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:34.060
Good question.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:37.720
The basic energy source for the sun comes from nuclear fusion.

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:41.590
And this is when mass particles combine and tons of energy are released.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:45.720
The core, or innermost part of the sun, is made of hydrogen.

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:55.080
The sun is so dense and its size is so large that light released from the core takes about 100,000 years to make its way to the surface.

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:59.970
If the sun were to stop producing energy today, it would take 100,000 years.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.940
for significant effects to be felt at the Earth.

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:05.840
Scientists think there is enough hydrogen on the sun

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:09.600
to continue producing energy for another 7 billion years.

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:13.000
For many centuries, little was known about the sun.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:18.980
However, in the early 1600s, the Italian scientist Galileo

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:21.340
used a telescope to take a closer look at the sun.

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:25.320
He found dark spots that occasionally appeared and drifted across the sun.

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:29.580
He also noticed that the dark spots on the sun's surface were constantly changing.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:31.340
These are called sunspots.

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:33.240
What are sunspots?

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:34.560
Let's find out.

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:38.860
NASA Goddard's Dr. Eric Christian has some answers for us at the Naval Observatory.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:39.940
It's a blast.

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:43.700
Thanks, Dan.

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:46.760
The sun is a fascinating place and a brilliant object to observe.

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:52.140
We observe the sun through telescopes like this one here at the Naval Observatory in Washington, D.C.

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:54.120
But satellites help us, too.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:57.400
To get a better understanding of the sun, let's look at its different parts.

00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:01.550
The visible surface of the sun,

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.820
that which we can actually see with the human eye,

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:06.150
is called the photosphere.

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:09.000
Temperatures here are around 6,000 degrees Celsius.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:12.700
The next two outer layers of the sun's atmosphere

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:14.400
are called the coromosphere and the corona.

00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:17.070
Corona is actually hotter than the photosphere,

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:19.480
temperatures of one to two million degrees Celsius.

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:22.370
The corona is visible to the naked eye

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:23.430
during solar eclipses.

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:26.530
Remember the dark spots, or sunspots,

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:27.890
that Galileo studied with his telescope?

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:29.890
Well, sunspots are dark.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.440
cool areas of the sun's surface where charged particles are emitted. The sunspot only looks

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:39.330
dark relative to the brightness of the rest of the sun, but it's still pretty hot, 4,000

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:44.770
degrees Celsius hot. The average sunspot is about the same diameter of the Earth. Sunspots

00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:49.900
generate some of the most violent storms in the solar system. When a sunspot erupts, we

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:54.760
call this a solar flare. Solar flares are some of the biggest explosions in the solar

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:59.920
system. When a solar flare occurs, gas heat of more than tens of thousands

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.880
of degrees and energy surpassing billions of atomic bombs is hurled out from the sun.

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:09.800
Another type of explosion is the CME, or coronal mass ejection.

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:14.690
These explosions can reach speeds of millions of kilometers per hour and can reach the Earth

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:15.650
in just three days.

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:23.550
Both solar flares and CMEs can be very disruptive to human activity on Earth and in space as

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:26.750
these storms, we call them solar storms, travel to the Earth.
